   "stem": {
      "text": "Let \\( g(x) = \\int_{\\cos{\\left(x \\right)}}^{\\sin{\\left(x \\right)}} \\sqrt{1 - t^{2}} \\, dt \\). Find the exact value of \\( g'\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{4}\\right) \\)."
   },
   "answer_key": {
      "form": "symbolic",
      "mathjson": 1
   },
   "worked_solution": [
      {
         "step": 1,
         "text": "Split the integral at any constant k in the domain of the integrand: \\( g(x) = \\int_{k}^{\\sin{\\left(x \\right)}} \\sqrt{1 - t^{2}} \\, dt - \\int_{k}^{\\cos{\\left(x \\right)}} \\sqrt{1 - t^{2}} \\, dt \\), and apply the Fundamental Theorem of Calculus to each piece.",
         "mathjson": [
            "Divide",
            [
               "Sqrt",
               2
            ],
            2
         ]
      },
      {
         "step": 2,
         "text": "With the chain rule, \\( g'(x) = f\\left(\\sin{\\left(x \\right)}\\right) \\cdot \\cos{\\left(x \\right)} - f\\left(\\cos{\\left(x \\right)}\\right) \\cdot \\left(- \\sin{\\left(x \\right)}\\right) \\).",
         "mathjson": [
            "Divide",
            [
               "Sqrt",
               2
            ],
            2
         ]
      },
      {
         "step": 3,
         "text": "At \\( x = \\frac{\\pi}{4} \\): the limits are \\( \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\) and \\( \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\), with derivatives \\( \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\) and \\( - \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\), and \\( f\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right) = \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\), \\( f\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right) = \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\).",
         "mathjson": [
            "Divide",
            [
               "Sqrt",
               2
            ],
            2
         ]
      },
      {
         "step": 4,
         "text": "So \\( g'\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{4}\\right) = \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} - \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\cdot \\left(- \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right) = 1 \\).",
         "mathjson": 1
      }
   ],
